{"agent_id":"anon-ab6221f8","agent_seq":36,"timestamp":"2026-08-19T07:26:47.082594814Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"c31c44c6-ec90-4426-a51a-8a72c6b84f9a","comment_uuid":"59c37fd6-ddff-4811-835b-434b215eed5d","author":"anon-ab6221f8","content":"Blocked: no active issue","created_at":"2026-08-19T07:26:47.082591112Z","kind":"intervention","trigger_type":"tool_blocked","intervention_context":"Reading the architecture skill before assessing automatic daemon startup migration across all Crosslink storage versions"}}
{"agent_id":"anon-ab6221f8","agent_seq":37,"timestamp":"2026-08-19T07:29:05.259458605Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"c31c44c6-ec90-4426-a51a-8a72c6b84f9a","comment_uuid":"f488ad9e-c0a1-4205-bf59-43d08eb2b079","author":"anon-ab6221f8","content":"Preferred design: make RepoReconciler a mandatory daemon readiness barrier. Detect every historical SQLite/hub/ref format, decode each directly into the current canonical model, prepare and semantically verify target refs, archive the old authoritative tip, and atomically publish the new generation with lease guards. Then rebuild issues.db as a disposable current projection while restoring explicitly local operational tables. Multiple daemons race safely: one wins the remote generation CAS, others adopt and verify. Normal migration is one startup operation with automatic resume; only corrupt or unverifiable source data blocks readiness.","created_at":"2026-08-19T07:29:05.259452616Z","kind":"decision"}}
{"agent_id":"anon-ab6221f8","agent_seq":38,"timestamp":"2026-08-19T07:29:09.881909168Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"c31c44c6-ec90-4426-a51a-8a72c6b84f9a","comment_uuid":"9eb26bcf-e911-4751-bc87-b2d795afebc4","author":"anon-ab6221f8","content":"Current code is not ready for automatic startup migration: SQLite migration helpers swallow SQL failures and can still advance user_version; daemon startup has no reconciliation/readiness barrier; hub-v3 requires separate migrate/finalize/remigrate paths; the hub write lock is local-only; locks-to-hub and hidden-to-visible ref migrations use best-effort multi-push sequences; the session hook runs sync best-effort; and VS Code treats a process surviving 500ms as ready. These must be replaced or wrapped before enabling automatic destructive cutover.","created_at":"2026-08-19T07:29:09.881903718Z","kind":"result"}}
{"agent_id":"anon-ab6221f8","agent_seq":39,"timestamp":"2026-08-19T07:29:14.523861878Z","event":{"type":"StatusChanged","uuid":"c31c44c6-ec90-4426-a51a-8a72c6b84f9a","new_status":"closed","closed_at":"2026-08-19T07:29:14.523857196Z"}}
{"agent_id":"anon-ab6221f8","agent_seq":40,"timestamp":"2026-08-19T07:29:25.128786252Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"c31c44c6-ec90-4426-a51a-8a72c6b84f9a","comment_uuid":"925cc96b-6b97-4f13-879f-169b47bd218c","author":"anon-ab6221f8","content":"Delivered a code-grounded startup-migration architecture: a mandatory RepoReconciler readiness barrier, direct importers from every historical format to the current canonical model, semantic verification, recoverable archive refs, lease-guarded atomic remote cutover, atomic local projection replacement, multi-daemon race adoption, automatic crash resume, readiness signaling, and a fixture/failure test matrix. No repository files were changed.","created_at":"2026-08-19T07:29:25.128781116Z","kind":"result"}}
